<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="lc-hl-calculus">
  <title>Calculus</title>
  <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol><li><p>Recognise a composite function and identify its component functions.</p></li><li><p>Differentiate composite functions using the chain rule.</p></li><li><p>Apply derivatives to rates of change, and connect these with earlier work on gradients.</p></li></ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Higher Level builds on Ordinary Level differentiation. Before proceeding, recall the power rule and <xref ref="lc-ol-stationary-points"/>. This chapter begins with composite functions; further differentiation rules and integration will be developed in this strand.</p><p><em>Curriculum connection:</em> Leaving Certificate Mathematics, Strand 5, topic 5.2 Calculus: differentiation of compositions of functions. See the <url href="https://www.curriculumonline.ie/senior-cycle/senior-cycle-subjects/mathematics/" visual="official syllabus"/>.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-composite-functions">
    <title>Composite Functions</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Identify the inner and outer functions in a composite expression.</p></li><li><p>Express a composition using function notation.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <definition xml:id="lc-hl-definition-composition"><title>Composite Function</title><statement><p><idx>composite function</idx>If the output of <m>g</m> lies in the domain of <m>f</m>, then their composition is <m>(f\circ g)(x)=f(g(x))</m>. Apply <m>g</m> first and <m>f</m> second.</p></statement></definition>
    <example><title>Two Functions in Succession</title><p>Consider <m>y=(3x+2)^4</m>. First calculate <m>u=3x+2</m>, then calculate <m>y=u^4</m>.</p><me>x\longrightarrow u=3x+2\longrightarrow y=u^4</me><p>The inner function is <m>g(x)=3x+2</m>; the outer function is <m>f(u)=u^4</m>.</p></example>
    <exercise><statement><p>Identify the inner and outer functions in <m>y=\sin(x^2+1)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p>Inner: <m>u=x^2+1</m>. Outer: <m>y=\sin u</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule">
    <title>The Chain Rule</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>State the chain rule in Leibniz and function notation.</p></li><li><p>Differentiate the outer function and multiply by the derivative of the inner function.</p></li><li><p>Check a derivative by expanding when practical.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <p>How do we differentiate <m>y=(3x+2)^4</m> without expanding four brackets? The chain rule connects the two stages of the calculation.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="lc-hl-theorem-chain-rule"><title>Chain Rule</title><statement><p><idx>chain rule</idx><idx><h>differentiation</h><h>chain rule</h></idx>If <m>g</m> is differentiable at <m>x</m> and <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>g(x)</m>, then</p><me>\frac{d}{dx}f(g(x))=f'(g(x))g'(x).</me><p>Equivalently, if <m>y=f(u)</m> and <m>u=g(x)</m>, then</p><me>\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}.</me></statement></theorem>
    <p>In words: differentiate the outside, keep the inside unchanged, and multiply by the derivative of the inside.</p>
    <example xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule-linear-power"><title>A Linear Expression Raised to a Power</title><p>Differentiate <m>y=(3x+2)^4</m>.</p><p>Let <m>u=3x+2</m>. Then <m>y=u^4</m>.</p><md><mrow>\frac{dy}{du}&amp;=4u^3</mrow><mrow>\frac{du}{dx}&amp;=3</mrow><mrow>\frac{dy}{dx}&amp;=4u^3(3)</mrow><mrow>&amp;=12(3x+2)^3.</mrow></md></example>
    <example><title>A Quadratic Inner Function</title><p>Differentiate <m>y=(x^2+1)^5</m>.</p><md><mrow>\frac{dy}{dx}&amp;=5(x^2+1)^4(2x)</mrow><mrow>&amp;=10x(x^2+1)^4.</mrow></md></example>
    <example><title>Check by Expansion</title><p>Let <m>y=(2x+3)^2</m>. By the chain rule, <m>y'=4(2x+3)=8x+12</m>. Expanding first gives <m>y=4x^2+12x+9</m>, and differentiating gives the same result.</p></example>
    <p><em>Common error:</em> Writing <m>4(3x+2)^3</m> for the first example omits the derivative of the inner function.</p>
    <exercise><statement><p>Differentiate <m>y=(5x-1)^3</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>15(5x-1)^2</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><statement><p>Differentiate <m>y=(2x^2-3)^4</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>16x(2x^2-3)^3</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule-diagrams">
    <title>The Chain Rule via Dependency Diagrams</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Represent a composition as a sequence of dependent variables.</p></li><li><p>Multiply the derivatives along the path.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <p>For <m>y=(3x+2)^4</m>, read the dependency diagram from the independent variable to the dependent variable.</p>
    <me>x\xrightarrow{\frac{du}{dx}=3}u\xrightarrow{\frac{dy}{du}=4u^3}y</me>
    <p>Each arrow records the local rate of change. Multiply the rates along the path to obtain the overall rate.</p>
    <example><title>Three Stages</title><p>Let <m>u=2x+1</m>, <m>v=u^2</m> and <m>y=\sin v</m>.</p><me>x\xrightarrow{2}u\xrightarrow{2u}v\xrightarrow{\cos v}y</me><p>Hence <m>y'=4(2x+1)\cos((2x+1)^2)</m>.</p></example>
    <exercise><statement><p>Use a dependency diagram to differentiate <m>y=e^{(x+1)^2}</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>2(x+1)e^{(x+1)^2}</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <p><em>Explore further (optional):</em> Branching dependency trees for functions of several independent variables belong to multivariable calculus, beyond the present Leaving Certificate treatment.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule-other-functions">
    <title>Trigonometric, Exponential and Logarithmic Functions</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Apply the chain rule to standard trigonometric, exponential and logarithmic functions.</p></li><li><p>Recognise the domain restrictions when differentiating.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <example><title>Trigonometric Function</title><p>For <m>y=\sin(5x)</m>, with angles measured in radians, <m>y'=5\cos(5x)</m>.</p></example>
    <example><title>Exponential Function</title><p>For <m>y=e^{x^2}</m>, <m>y'=2xe^{x^2}</m>.</p></example>
    <example><title>Logarithmic Function</title><p>For <m>y=\ln(2x+1)</m>, where <m>x&gt;-\frac12</m>,</p><me>y'=\frac{2}{2x+1}.</me></example>
    <example><title>Square Root</title><p>For <m>y=\sqrt{3x+4}</m>, at points where <m>3x+4&gt;0</m>,</p><me>y'=\frac{3}{2\sqrt{3x+4}}.</me></example>
    <exercise><statement><p>Differentiate <m>y=\cos(4x)</m>, <m>y=e^{3x-1}</m> and <m>y=\ln(x^2+1)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-4\sin(4x)</m>, <m>3e^{3x-1}</m> and <m>\frac{2x}{x^2+1}</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule-rates">
    <title>Applications: Rates of Change</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Relate rates of change between quantities.</p></li><li><p>Interpret the numerical answer with appropriate units.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <example><title>An Expanding Circle</title><p>A circular region has radius <m>r=2t+1</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds. Its area is <m>A=\pi r^2</m>. Find how fast its area is increasing when <m>t=3</m>.</p><md><mrow>\frac{dA}{dt}&amp;=\frac{dA}{dr}\frac{dr}{dt}</mrow><mrow>&amp;=2\pi r(2)</mrow><mrow>&amp;=4\pi r.</mrow></md><p>At <m>t=3</m>, <m>r=7</m> and the area increases at <m>28\pi</m> square metres per second.</p></example>
    <exercise><statement><p>A square has side length <m>s=3t+2</m> cm. Find the rate at which its area increases at <m>t=2</m> seconds.</p></statement><answer><p><m>48</m> square centimetres per second.</p></answer></exercise>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="lc-hl-chain-rule-practice">
    <title>Independent Practice</title>
    <objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Select and apply the chain rule independently.</p></li><li><p>Diagnose missing inner derivatives and check answers.</p></li></ol></objectives>
    <exercise><title>Easy</title><statement><p>Differentiate <m>(2x+1)^6</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>12(2x+1)^5</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><title>Easy</title><statement><p>Differentiate <m>(7-3x)^4</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-12(7-3x)^3</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><title>Intermediate</title><statement><p>Differentiate <m>\sqrt{x^2+4}</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+4}}</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><title>Intermediate</title><statement><p>Differentiate <m>\cos(x^2-1)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>-2x\sin(x^2-1)</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><title>Challenging</title><statement><p>Differentiate <m>\ln((2x+1)^2+3)</m>.</p></statement><answer><p><m>\frac{4(2x+1)}{(2x+1)^2+3}</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
    <exercise><title>Find the Error</title><statement><p>A student writes <m>\frac{d}{dx}(4x-1)^5=5(4x-1)^4</m>. Identify the error and correct it.</p></statement><answer><p>The inner derivative <m>4</m> is missing. The correct derivative is <m>20(4x-1)^4</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
